       and it is not capturable at executable prices.

============================================================================================
TEST 3 -- EXECUTABLE EDGE ON THE GATED TAIL (|dev_last| > 10c)
============================================================================================
  The cost gate does not trade the average basket, it trades the tail.
  So ask the tail the executable question directly, with no mid and no
  cross-leg averaging: a basket priced ABOVE $1 is SOLD, which means
  hitting the bid on every leg; one priced BELOW $1 is BOUGHT at the ask.
  Either way it settles at exactly $1.00.

  baskets the gate would fire on : 37 (5.6% of two-sided baskets)
    of which SOLD (sum > $1)     : 32
    of which BOUGHT (sum < $1)   : 5

  GROSS edge at executable prices, before any fee:
    median  +1.00c per basket
    mean    -4.08c
    share positive  54.1%
    share above 5c  24.3%

  This is the whole PnL question in one number. A gross edge at or
  below zero means the gate's deviations are not capturable at the
  prices actually available, whatever the last-trade series shows,
  and no fee model can rescue it. A clearly positive gross edge means
  the tail is real and fees decide the rest.

  For contrast, the SAME calculation on all 662 two-sided
  baskets: median +1.00c, share positive 84.0%
